Subject: [PATCH] keyboard: replay the layout map from the profile

navigator.keyboard.getLayoutMap() returns what each physical key produces. The
reference M4 Max reports 48 entries; ours reported 48 too, but two disagreed:
IntlBackslash was "<" where the Mac says "\u00a7", and we reported an IntlYen
key the Mac does not have at all.

Both are the build host's X keymap showing through. The section sign on
IntlBackslash is specific to Apple's ISO layout, and IntlYen exists on Japanese
keyboards, so the pair together describes a machine that is neither the Mac the
rest of the capture claims nor any single real keyboard.

Replayed rather than derived, which is the axiom A3 category for this. A layout
map is a user setting, not a device property — the same Mac produces a
different map under a German layout — and nothing else in the profile implies
it, so there is no rule that could generate it. The capture is the only source.

Replaced wholesale rather than merged. A layout is one coherent object, and
taking most keys from the reference while leaving the rest to the host's keymap
would describe a keyboard that does not exist. Merging is how the two-field
version of this bug was produced in the first place.

Patched at PageImpl::GetKeyboardLayoutMap, which is the only path by which a
layout reaches a renderer: KeyboardLockServiceImpl reads it there and nowhere
else, so the accessor never needs touching.

Iteration order is not a new observable. The return type is a flat_map on every
platform, so whatever ordering a page eventually sees is produced downstream
from the key set, identically for us and for stock Chrome. Fixing the key set
is therefore sufficient; no ordering work is required or attempted.

An empty map in the profile means "leave the host's map alone", so a profile
that does not describe a keyboard does not silently blank one.

Ledger: keyboard.layout-map.
diff --git a/base/apostate/profile.cc b/base/apostate/profile.cc
index 54ca7a3..d0e6938 100644
--- a/base/apostate/profile.cc
+++ b/base/apostate/profile.cc
@@ -216,6 +216,19 @@ Profile* Profile::ParseOrNull(const std::string& encoded) {
     }
   }
 
+  if (const DictValue* keyboard = dict->FindDict("keyboard")) {
+    if (const DictValue* map = keyboard->FindDict("layout_map")) {
+      for (const auto [code, value] : *map) {
+        if (const std::string* s = value.GetIfString()) {
+          profile->keyboard_layout_map_[code] = *s;
+        } else {
+          LOG(ERROR) << "apostate: keyboard.layout_map." << code
+                     << " must be a string; ignoring";
+        }
+      }
+    }
+  }
+
   if (const DictValue* locale = dict->FindDict("locale")) {
     if (const std::string* v = locale->FindString("timezone")) {
       profile->timezone_ = *v;
diff --git a/base/apostate/profile.h b/base/apostate/profile.h
index 6e71f86..c482e2b 100644
--- a/base/apostate/profile.h
+++ b/base/apostate/profile.h
@@ -6,6 +6,7 @@
 #define BASE_APOSTATE_PROFILE_H_
 
 #include <cstdint>
+#include <map>
 #include <optional>
 #include <string>
 
@@ -179,6 +180,16 @@ class BASE_EXPORT Profile {
     return media_videoinput_count_;
   }
 
+  // navigator.keyboard.getLayoutMap(), replayed from the capture.
+  //
+  // This is a user setting rather than a device property — the same Mac gives a
+  // different map under a German layout — and it is not derivable from anything
+  // else in the profile, so axiom A3 puts it in the replay category rather than
+  // the derived one. Empty means "leave the host's map alone".
+  const std::map<std::string, std::string>& keyboard_layout_map() const {
+    return keyboard_layout_map_;
+  }
+
   // IANA timezone name, e.g. "Europe/Warsaw".
   //
   // Only the zone *name* is a host fact. ICU's data is compiled into the binary
@@ -261,6 +272,7 @@ class BASE_EXPORT Profile {
   std::optional<int> audio_hardware_buffer_frames_;
   std::optional<int> media_audioinput_count_;
   std::optional<int> media_videoinput_count_;
+  std::map<std::string, std::string> keyboard_layout_map_;
   std::optional<std::string> timezone_;
   std::optional<bool> prefers_dark_;
   std::optional<std::string> accept_languages_;
diff --git a/content/browser/renderer_host/page_impl.cc b/content/browser/renderer_host/page_impl.cc
index edbcafa..5770ed8 100644
--- a/content/browser/renderer_host/page_impl.cc
+++ b/content/browser/renderer_host/page_impl.cc
@@ -4,6 +4,8 @@
 
 #include "content/browser/renderer_host/page_impl.h"
 
+#include "base/apostate/profile.h"
+
 #include "base/barrier_closure.h"
 #include "base/feature_list.h"
 #include "base/i18n/character_encoding.h"
@@ -361,6 +363,26 @@ void PageImpl::SetVirtualKeyboardMode(ui::mojom::VirtualKeyboardMode mode) {
 }
 
 base::flat_map<std::string, std::string> PageImpl::GetKeyboardLayoutMap() {
+  // Replayed from the profile when it carries a map. This is the one place the
+  // layout reaches a renderer — KeyboardLockServiceImpl reads it here and
+  // nowhere else — so overriding it here covers navigator.keyboard.getLayoutMap()
+  // without touching the accessor.
+  //
+  // The host's map is replaced wholesale rather than merged. A layout is a
+  // single coherent object: taking most keys from a US Mac and the rest from
+  // whatever X keymap the build host happens to have would describe a keyboard
+  // that does not exist, which is the failure the axioms exist to prevent.
+  //
+  // Iteration order is not a new observable. This return type is a flat_map on
+  // every platform, so the ordering a page finally sees is produced downstream
+  // from the key set, identically for us and for stock Chrome.
+  if (const base::apostate::Profile* profile = base::apostate::Profile::Get()) {
+    const std::map<std::string, std::string>& map =
+        profile->keyboard_layout_map();
+    if (!map.empty()) {
+      return base::flat_map<std::string, std::string>(map.begin(), map.end());
+    }
+  }
   return GetMainDocument().GetRenderWidgetHost()->GetKeyboardLayoutMap();
 }
 
